```javascript
const { SlackBot } = require('slackbot');

function postMessageToChannel(text, channel) {
  const bot = new SlackBot({
    token: 'YOUR_SLACKBOT_TOKEN',
    name: 'Your Bot Name',
    autoMarkdown: true,
    retry: Infinity,
  });

  bot.start();

  bot.postMessageToChannel(channel, text);

  setTimeout(() => {
    bot.stop();
  }, 1000); // Stop the bot after one second to prevent unnecessary resource usage
}

// Example usage:
postMessageToChannel('Hello, world!', '#general');
```

In this implementation, you'll need to replace `YOUR_SLACKBOT_TOKEN` with your actual Slack bot token, and adjust the `bot.name` value to match the name you registered when creating the bot. The `autoMarkdown` option is set to `true`, which will automatically convert Markdown syntax (such as asterisks for bold text or hashtags for links) into their respective formats.

The `postMessageToChannel()` method accepts two arguments - the text you want to post and the name of the channel you want to post it to (in this case, `#general`). Note that the channel name must be prefixed with a `#`.

This function returns immediately, but the bot object is started asynchronously and the `postMessageToChannel()` call is made synchronously. This allows us to stop the bot after posting the message to avoid unnecessary resource usage.

Note that this implementation assumes that you have already created a Slack bot and obtained its token. If you haven't done so yet, you can follow the instructions provided by Slack here: https://api.slack.com/docs/oauth-test-token

