To blend multiple audio files using Java, you can use a library called SoX (Simple Operations on Audio Files). Here's how you can do it:

1. First, download and install SoX for your operating system from their official website.

2. Create a new Java project in your preferred Integrated Development Environment (IDE) such as Eclipse, IntelliJ IDEA or NetBeans.

3. Add the SoX jar file to your project's build path. You can find the jar file in the directory where you installed SoX.

4. Write the following Java code to blend your audio files:

```java
import org.sox4j.*;
import java.io.*;

public class AudioBlender {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Sox sox = new Sox();

        // Load input audio files
        InputAudio input1 = new FileInputAudio("birds.wav");
        InputAudio input2 = new FileInputAudio("leaves.wav");
        InputAudio input3 = new FileInputAudio("cicadas.wav");
        InputAudio input4 = new FileInputAudio("children_playing.wav");

        // Set output format and bitrate
        OutputFormat format = new OutputFormat("AU", 48000);

        // Mix inputs with equal weights
        int channels = 2;
        float volume = 0.5f;
        sox.setOption("-m", String.valueOf(channels));
        sox.setOption("-V", String.valueOf(volume));
        sox.buildChain()
                .addSource(input1)
                .addSource(input2)
                .addSource(input3)
                .addSource(input4)
                .setOutput(new FileOutputAudio("summer_day.wav"))
                .setFormat(format)
                .chain();

        // Execute chain and wait for completion
        sox.run();

        System.out.println("Audio blending complete.");
    }
}
```

