const sortedKeys = Object.entries(obj).sort(([keyA], [keyB]) => {
  // You can customize the compare function based on your requirements
  // Return -1 if keyA comes before keyB, 0 if they're equal, and 1 otherwise
  return ["a", "b", "c"].indexOf(keyA) - ["a", "b", "c"].indexOf(keyB);
});

// Convert the sorted array back to an object using Object.fromEntries()
const sortedObj = Object.fromEntries(sortedKeys);
console.log(sortedObj);
```

In this example, we're defining a simple compare function that sorts the keys based on their lexicographical order (i.e., A, B, C), but you can modify it according to your specific needs.

Note that this implementation assumes that all keys will be present in the input object. If you need to handle missing keys or different data types for values, you may need to adapt the code accordingly.

