To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in WordPress when using Next.js, you can follow these steps:

1. Install `cors` middleware in your Next.js app by running `npm install cors` or `yarn add cors`.
2. Add the following code to your `pages/_app.js` file:

```javascript
import Cors from 'cors';
const cors = new Cors({ origin: 'https://your-wordpress-site.com' });

function MyApp({ Component, pageProps }) {
  return (
    <>
      <Component {...pageProps} />
    </>
  );
}

export default cors(MyApp);
```

Replace `https://your-wordpress-site.com` with the URL of your WordPress site. This sets up the `cors` middleware and allows requests from that specific domain.

3. In your WordPress site, go to the `functions.php` file in your theme folder or a plugin file, and add the following code:

```javascript
add_action('wp_head', function() {
   header('Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true');
   header('Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE');
   header('Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization, Content-Type');
}, 10);
```

This adds necessary headers to allow credentials, methods, and headers for CORS requests.

With these steps, your Next.js app should be able to make CORS requests to your WordPress site without any errors. Remember to adjust the `origin` value in `pages/_app.js` to match the domain where your Next.js app will be served from.

